p attached? Actually the parentheses are attached to the preceding atom, which is the nitrogen? Let\'s parse: "CNC(C)" - the string "CNC" is three atoms: C, N, C. Then "(C)" means that the preceding atom (the last C) has a branch: an additional C attached. So the group is CH3-N-CH(CH3)-. So it\'s a secondary amine with methyl and ethyl (with methyl branch) substituents? Actually it\'s N-methyl-1-aminopropane? Wait, it\'s CH3-NH-CH(CH3)-? But nitrogen has two carbons attached: one methyl (first C), one ethyl (with methyl branch). So it\'s N-methyl-1-aminopropane (isopropylamine derivative). So the molecule is (N-methyl-1-aminopropyl)benzene with two trifluoromethyl groups on the benzene ring.\\n\\nNow, skin sensitization is often associated with electrophilic reactivity, ability to form covalent bonds with skin proteins. Chemicals that are skin sensitizers often are electrophiles or can be metabolized to electrophiles. They may have Michael acceptors, aldehydes, epoxides, isocyanates, etc. This molecule appears to be an amine with trifluoromethyl groups. Amines can be sensitizers, especially if they are primary amines or can form reactive metabolites. However, secondary amines might be less reactive. Trifluoromethyl groups are electron-withdrawing, which might affect reactivity.\\n\\nI need to use tools to compute molecular properties that might correlate with skin sensitization. There\'s no direct skin sensitization predictor, but I can compute properties like logP, polar surface area, H-bond donors/acceptors, etc. Maybe I can use structural alerts for skin sensitization. There\'s a tool "score_structural_alerts" that can screen against various alert libraries (PAINS, Brenk, NIH, ZINC, ChEMBL). Skin sensitization might be captured in some of these alerts. I can also compute other descriptors like electrophilicity indices? Not directly available. There\'s "get_max_abs_partial_charge" and "get_min_abs_partial_charge" which might indicate partial charges that could cor